---
import DocShell from '../layouts/doc-shell.astro';
import { docsLinks } from '../lib/nav';

const toc = [
   { id: 'supported', label: 'Supported screen readers' },
   { id: 'default', label: 'Which one runs without --sr' },
   { id: 'virtual', label: 'Using the simulated screen reader' },
   { id: 'real', label: 'Using VoiceOver or NVDA' },
   { id: 'choose', label: 'Which one to use' },
];
---

<DocShell
   title="Screen readers"
   summary="a11ied drives three screen readers: VoiceOver on macOS, NVDA on Windows, and a simulated screen reader that runs on any platform. --sr picks one, and every result says which one ran."
   links={docsLinks}
   toc={toc}
>
   <section class="ledger" id="supported">
      <h2>Supported screen readers</h2>
      <div class="route-list">
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>voiceover</code>
            <span>
               macOS. Drives VoiceOver through Guidepup and returns what it announces. Can record
               the screen to a <code>.mov</code> file.
            </span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>nvda</code>
            <span>
               Windows. Drives NVDA through Guidepup and returns what it announces. Can record the
               screen to an <code>.mp4</code> file.
            </span>
         </div>
         <div class="route-row">
            <code>virtual</code>
            <span>
               Any platform. A screen reader implemented in software that runs inside a headless
               Chromium page and produces the same kind of announcements as VoiceOver or NVDA, with
               neither installed. Nothing opens on screen, so it cannot record. When no
               Chromium-family browser is installed, or the session has no URL, it reads a jsdom
               document instead, where page scripts do not run.
            </span>
         </div>
      </div>
      <p>
         Every result from an <code>a1 sr</code> command says which screen reader ran, in the
         <code>target</code> field, and says whether it was VoiceOver or NVDA or the simulation in
         <code>targetType</code>: <code>real</code> or <code>simulated</code>. The simulated screen
         reader also reports <code>engine</code>: <code>browser</code> for the headless Chromium
         page, <code>jsdom</code> for the in-memory document.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="default">
      <h2>Which one runs without --sr</h2>
      <p>a11ied picks a screen reader from the operating system, then checks that it is set up:</p>
      <ol>
         <li>macOS picks <code>voiceover</code>. Windows picks <code>nvda</code>.</li>
         <li>Linux and every other platform pick <code>virtual</code>.</li>
         <li>
            When VoiceOver or NVDA is installed but <code>a1 doctor</code> reports it as
            <code>requires-setup</code>, the session runs <code>virtual</code> instead and adds a
            warning to the result that says why.
         </li>
      </ol>
      <p>
         The warning on that fallback says the screen reader is simulated and its output does not
         show how VoiceOver or NVDA behaves. <code>a1 doctor</code> prints each screen reader as
         <code>ready</code> or <code>requires-setup</code>, with the command that fixes it.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="virtual">
      <h2>Using the simulated screen reader</h2>
      <p>
         On macOS and Windows, <code>--sr virtual</code> alone exits 2 with the error
         <code>virtual-target-disallowed</code>. Add <code>--allow-virtual</code> to confirm you
         want the simulation instead of VoiceOver or NVDA.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr virtual --allow-virtual http://localhost:3000/checkout --json</code></pre>
      <p>
         Pass the URL so the screen reader has a page to read. The session loads the page in a
         headless Chromium page and runs the screen reader inside it, so the page's own scripts
         run: a live region a script fills, or a dialog a click opens, is announced.
         <code>a1 sr status</code> prints the engine, and <code>session.engine</code> has it
         in every JSON result. An installed Chrome, Edge, Brave, or Chromium is used first, then
         the Chromium that <code>npx playwright install chromium</code> downloads.
      </p>
      <p>
         Two cases use jsdom, an in-memory document where page scripts do not run: a session
         started without a URL, which reads a placeholder document until <code>sr open</code>,
         and a machine with no Chromium-family browser, where the session reports
         <code>engine: "jsdom"</code>. Inline HTML passed to the test API renders in jsdom too.
         Because it opens no window, the simulated screen reader runs in CI and in a watch task on
         a developer machine.
      </p>
      <p>
         MCP clients pass the same two fields: <code>target: "virtual"</code> and
         <code>allowVirtual: true</code>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="panel" id="real">
      <h2>Using VoiceOver or NVDA</h2>
      <p>
         VoiceOver and NVDA read whatever is on screen. Give <code>sr start</code> the URL and it
         opens the page in the system browser, waits for that window to come to the front, and
         then starts the screen reader.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 sr start --sr voiceover http://localhost:3000/checkout --json
a1 sr next --json</code></pre>
      <p>
         <code>sr start</code> records which app it opened. A bare <code>a1 sr focus</code> brings
         that app back to the front later. <code>sr focus</code> also accepts <code>--app</code> or
         <code>--bundle-id</code> on macOS, <code>--process</code> or <code>--pid</code> on
         Windows, and <code>--window-title</code> on either.
      </p>
      <pre><code>a1 setup</code></pre>
      <p>
         Run it once per machine before the first VoiceOver or NVDA session. On macOS it runs
         <code>npx -y @guidepup/setup setup</code>, which turns on VoiceOver's AppleScript
         control, then <code>npx -y @guidepup/setup install</code>, which downloads the VoiceOver
         preferences bundle every session mounts. On Windows only the install step runs, and it
         downloads the NVDA build.
      </p>
      <p>
         <code>a1 setup</code> ends by printing the <code>doctor</code> report, so you can see
         every check pass. Add <code>--skip-setup</code> or <code>--skip-install</code> to run one
         step only. Screen recording on macOS needs one more permission: whichever app runs
         <code>a1</code> needs Screen Recording access in System Settings. See
         <a href="/guides/recording">record a session</a>.
      </p>
   </section>

   <section class="field-note" id="choose">
      <h2>Which one to use</h2>
      <p>
         Use <code>virtual</code> to check the order elements are announced in, and the name,
         role, and state each one announces with. Its output is the same on every run and it opens
         no window, so it fits a watch task, a pre-commit hook, and a CI job.
      </p>
      <p>
         Use <code>voiceover</code> or <code>nvda</code> when the question is how that screen
         reader behaves: its rotor or elements list, its interaction mode inside a group or
         table, where its cursor lands when a dialog opens or closes, and anything you plan to
         record or quote in a bug report.
      </p>
   </section>
</DocShell>
